--TEST--
An `abstract` class has no instances, and a class that is not `abstract` declares no bodiless method
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `abstract` says a class leaves members open for a subclass to fill in, so
// there is no object of it to allocate: `new` on one is refused where it is
// written, which is where PHP refuses it too.
abstract class Shape {
    public abstract function area(): int;

    public function describe(): string {
        return "area=" . $this->area();
    }

    // `new static()` is *not* refused here: late static binding resolves it
    // to whichever concrete subclass the call arrived through, which is what
    // an abstract factory method is for.
    public static function make(): static {
        return new static();
    }
}

class Square extends Shape {
    public function constructor(public int $side) {}

    public function area(): int {
        return $this->side * $this->side;
    }
}

// Legal — the subclass gives every open member a body.
Shape $ok = new Square(3);
echo $ok->describe(), "\n";

// Refused: `Shape::area` has nothing behind it, so an instance of `Shape`
// could not answer a call its own type admits.
Shape $bad = new Shape();

// The other direction of the same rule, refused at the declaration: a class
// that is not `abstract` may not leave a method without a body, because a
// call to one names no compiled function and dies as a `FATAL` at run time.
class Counter {
    public abstract function reset(): void;
}
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0785]: `Shape` is `abstract`, so it has no instances
%A
error[E0786]: `Counter::reset` is `abstract`, but `Counter` is not
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
